Now we reach the most important SQL topic for interviews and real projects:

JOINS

A JOIN combines data from multiple tables.

Why Do We Need JOINS?

Suppose we have two tables.

Students
student_id	name
1	Nanda
2	Ravi
3	Sai
Enrollments
enroll_id	student_id	course
101	1	SQL
102	2	Java
103	1	Python

Question:

Which student enrolled in which course?

The answer is spread across TWO tables.

We need a JOIN.

INNER JOIN

The most commonly used JOIN.

Returns only matching rows from both tables.

Syntax
SELECT columns
FROM table1
INNER JOIN table2
ON table1.column = table2.column;
Example
SELECT Students.name, Enrollments.course
FROM Students
INNER JOIN Enrollments
ON Students.student_id = Enrollments.student_id;
Step-by-Step Matching
Students.student_id
         =
Enrollments.student_id
Visual
Students                Enrollments

1 Nanda            ↔     1 SQL
2 Ravi             ↔     2 Java
3 Sai              ↔     No Match
Output
name	course
Nanda	SQL
Ravi	Java
Nanda	Python

Notice:

Sai is not shown because there is no enrollment record.

LEFT JOIN

Returns:

All rows from LEFT table
Matching rows from RIGHT table
Syntax
SELECT *
FROM Students
LEFT JOIN Enrollments
ON Students.student_id = Enrollments.student_id;
Visual
Students                Enrollments

1 Nanda            ↔     1 SQL
2 Ravi             ↔     2 Java
3 Sai              ↔     No Match
Output
name	course
Nanda	SQL
Ravi	Java
Sai	NULL
Important

LEFT JOIN keeps all rows from the left table.

RIGHT JOIN

Returns:

All rows from RIGHT table
Matching rows from LEFT table

Suppose Enrollments contains:

enroll_id	student_id	course
101	1	SQL
102	2	Java
103	5	Python

Notice:

student_id = 5 doesn't exist in Students.

SELECT *
FROM Students
RIGHT JOIN Enrollments
ON Students.student_id = Enrollments.student_id;
Output
name	course
Nanda	SQL
Ravi	Java
NULL	Python
Quick Comparison
INNER JOIN
   ↓
Only Matches

LEFT JOIN
   ↓
All Left + Matches

RIGHT JOIN
   ↓
All Right + Matches
Interview Trick

Most interviewers ask:

Difference between INNER JOIN and LEFT JOIN?

INNER JOIN

Returns only matching records.

LEFT JOIN

Returns all records from left table and matching records from right table.

Visual Summary
Students
   ⟷
Enrollments

INNER JOIN
  → only common rows

LEFT JOIN
  → all students

RIGHT JOIN
  → all enrollments
Real Project Example (Event Planner App)

You are building:

Users Table
user_id	name
1	Nanda
2	Ravi
Events Table
event_id	user_id	title
101	1	Tech Fest
102	2	Workshop

To display:

Nanda → Tech Fest
Ravi  → Workshop

you would use:

SELECT Users.name, Events.title
FROM Users
INNER JOIN Events
ON Users.user_id = Events.user_id;
